<h2>What a valid plea requires</h2>
<p>The entire weight of Section 416 rests on the integrity of the plea, which is why courts police the recording of it with care. A plea that carries the bar is one made by the accused personally, to a charge read over and explained, in the accused's own words rather than as a bare "yes", voluntarily and with understanding of the consequences. Pleas recorded through counsel where personal attendance was dispensed with, admissions of facts that do not amount to the offence charged, and "pleas" extracted by a promise of leniency all fall short — and a conviction built on such a plea is examinable notwithstanding the bar, because the condition of the bar was never met. The practical lesson for trial courts and defence counsel alike is to treat the plea stage as a recorded, deliberate event, not a formality.</p>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Plea bargaining is a different track.</strong> A mutually satisfactory disposition under the plea bargaining chapter of the BNSS carries its own, even stricter finality — the judgment is final and no appeal lies, subject only to the constitutional jurisdictions. An accused choosing between an open plea of guilty and a bargained disposition should understand that both close the ordinary appellate road, but the bargain also fixes the sentence in advance.</p>
</div>

<h2>The sentence appeal that survives</h2>
<p>For convictions by a Sessions Court or Magistrate on a plea, the surviving appeal is confined to the extent or legality of the sentence — and that is a real remedy, not a consolation. "Extent" reaches excessiveness: a sentence disproportionate to the offence and the offender, failure to consider probation where the statute permits it, or default terms out of line with the fine. "Legality" reaches sentences the court could not pass at all: terms beyond the statutory maximum, a sentence below a mandatory minimum improperly structured, or a form of punishment the provision does not authorise. The conviction stands; everything about the punishment remains arguable.</p>

<h2>Advising the plea decision</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Price the bar before the plea.</strong> The right to appeal the conviction is part of what is surrendered; weigh it against the realistic trial outcome and the sentencing benefit.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Build the sentence record.</strong> Since only the sentence remains appealable, the mitigation material — age, antecedents, family circumstances, restitution — must be on record at the sentencing hearing itself.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Check the petty-cases thresholds.</strong> A fine calibrated just under the Section 417 threshold extinguishes even the limited appeal; know where the sentence will land before consenting to anything.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Preserve the plea record.</strong> If the plea was defective, the certified record of what was read, explained and answered is the entire case in revision — obtain it immediately.</div>
</div>

<div class="check">
  <p>Summary: a true guilty plea converts the appellate contest into a sentencing contest. The accused's protections move upstream — into the framing and explanation of the charge, the voluntariness of the plea, and the sentencing hearing. Treat each of those stages as the appeal that will never be.</p>
</div>

